The code statements that lead to the bug are:
res = usb_submit_urb(async_urb, GFP_ATOMIC);
if (res) {
    if (res == -ENODEV)
        netif_device_detach(dev->netdev);
    dev_err(&dev->udev->dev, "%s failed with %d\n", __func__, res);
}
Reason:
When `usb_submit_urb()` fails, the error handling path does not free the previously allocated `req` (asynchronous request structure) and `async_urb` (URB). The cleanup was intended to be performed by the completion callback `async_set_reg_cb()`, but that callback is only invoked after a successful submission. Thus, on submission failure the memory is leaked.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
+		kfree(req);
+		usb_free_urb(async_urb);
Reason:
The added lines explicitly free the allocated request structure (`req`) and the URB (`async_urb`) inside the error path of `usb_submit_urb()` failure. This ensures that all allocated resources are released immediately when submission fails, preventing the memory leak.

